#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""One x86-metal boot, end to end (docs/metal/README.md, "One boot").

  metal-boot launch [run options]   start the driver as its own transient unit; prints {"id", "status"} once the
                                    unit holds or waits for the lease (exit 75 if busy with --no-wait, or quarantined)
  metal-boot run --id ID [options]  the driver itself (what the unit runs)
  metal-boot stop ID                ask a running boot to stop (exit 130 before power-on, 143 after)
  metal-boot inputs-ready ID        the image build for ID has delivered its inputs
  metal-boot inputs-failed ID WHY   the image build for ID failed
  metal-boot status                 the lease, the machine reading and the newest records, as JSON

# claim-lint:ok: #1680 Python None or CLI none is a source token, not an empirical quantifier.
Run options: --payload image|hang|none, --mode default|shell|probe|suite|console, --commit SHA, --target main|branch:NAME,
--task NAME, --no-wait, --inject forceoff-fail, --ramdisk, --disk install|root. A Breenix boot (payload image) waits
for its inputs (the kernel build's UEFI image, the ext2 root, the delivery stub and the commit's definition files) in
its stage folder, and serves boot.efi: the stub with the boot disk appended (the ext2 root joins the disk as
`ramdisk` by default). `--disk install` (probe mode) stages the install request on the RAM disk, so the kernel copies
it onto the machine's disk; `--disk root` (probe mode) delivers no RAM disk, so the kernel boots from the disk the
last install wrote (README, "The disks").
"""
